\chapter{Deterministic Runtime Specification for the VS-ACSE Engine}
\label{chap:vsacse_runtime_spec}

\section{Introduction}

This chapter defines the deterministic runtime model governing execution of the
VS-ACSE Hardware-Deterministic Autonomous Intelligence (DAI) Core. The runtime
specification formalizes the guarantees provided by the zero-allocation
Q16.16 fixed-point arithmetic substrate, AVX2 SIMD vector pipeline, and
ChaCha20/SHA3-256 cryptographic telemetry mesh during planetary-scale
simulation.

The runtime is designed to ensure absolute bit-level reproducibility across
heterogeneous hardware nodes, bounded latency envelopes, and tamper-evident
state propagation suitable for high-consequence scientific computation.

\section{Runtime Execution Model}

The VS-ACSE runtime operates under a strictly deterministic execution model
defined by the following invariants:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Zero Heap Allocation:} All memory is statically allocated
    and page-locked prior to execution. No dynamic allocation is permitted.
    \item \textbf{Fixed-Point Arithmetic:} All numerical operations use
    Q16.16 signed integers, eliminating IEEE 754 floating-point drift.
    \item \textbf{Vectorized State Evolution:} Simulation state is stored in
    256-bit AVX2 registers, enabling 8-lane parallel evolution per state vector.
    \item \textbf{Deterministic Control Flow:} No conditional branching based
    on runtime hardware state is permitted.
    \item \textbf{Cryptographic Integrity Loop:} Every epoch is hashed using
    SHA3-256 and validated against a deterministic ChaCha20 stream state.
\end{enumerate}

These invariants ensure that the runtime behaves identically across all
supported hardware configurations.

\section{State Representation}

The global simulation state at epoch $k$ is represented as:

\[
\mathbf{S}(t_k) =
\left(
\mathbf{P}_k,
\mathbf{C}_k,
\mathbf{T}_k,
\mathbf{R}_k
\right),
\]

where each component is a 256-bit AVX2 vector:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf{P}_k$: Mantle pressure vector
    \item $\mathbf{C}_k$: Crust stress vector
    \item $\mathbf{T}_k$: Thermal gradient vector
    \item $\mathbf{R}_k$: Rift velocity vector
\end{itemize}

All components evolve deterministically according to fixed-point arithmetic
and vectorized update rules.

\section{Deterministic Update Function}

The runtime defines a deterministic mapping:

\[
\Phi_{\text{VS-ACSE}} :
(\mathbf{S}(t_k), \Delta t_k)
\mapsto
\mathbf{S}(t_{k+1}),
\]

implemented via AVX2 intrinsics and fixed-point multiplication:

\[
\mathbf{P}_{k+1}
=
\mathbf{P}_k
+
\left[
(\mathbf{P}_k \cdot g) - (\mathbf{C}_k \cdot \nu)
\right]
\cdot \Delta t_k,
\]

\[
\mathbf{C}_{k+1}
=
\mathbf{C}_k + \mathbf{U},
\]

\[
\mathbf{T}_{k+1}
=
\mathbf{T}_k \oplus \left[
(\mathbf{P}_k \cdot g) - (\mathbf{C}_k \cdot \nu)
\right],
\]

\[
\mathbf{R}_{k+1}
=
\mathbf{P}_{k+1} \cdot g,
\]

where:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $g$: Gravity coefficient in Q16.16 format
    \item $\nu$: Viscosity decay coefficient
    \item $\mathbf{U}$: Control vector
\end{itemize}

All operations are performed using AVX2 32-bit lane arithmetic.

\section{Memory Determinism}

The runtime enforces strict memory determinism:

\begin{itemize}
    \item All state vectors reside in page-locked static memory.
    \item No heap allocation or deallocation occurs during execution.
    \item No pointer aliasing is permitted in the update function.
    \item All memory access patterns are fixed and compile-time defined.
\end{itemize}

This eliminates nondeterministic behavior caused by memory fragmentation,
garbage collection, or OS paging.

\section{Vector Lane Determinism}

Each AVX2 lane evolves independently but deterministically:

\[
\mathbf{P}_{k+1}[i] = f(\mathbf{P}_k[i], \mathbf{C}_k[i], \Delta t_k),
\]

for all lanes $i \in \{0,1,\dots,7\}$.

Lane alignment is guaranteed by:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{\_mm256\_blend\_epi32} for fixed-point multiplication
    \item \texttt{\_mm256\_add\_epi32} for state updates
    \item \texttt{\_mm256\_xor\_si256} for thermal gradient evolution
\end{itemize}

No cross-lane dependencies exist, ensuring reproducibility across hardware.

\section{Cryptographic Telemetry Specification}

Each epoch produces a cryptographically bound state snapshot:

\[
H_k = \text{SHA3-256}(S_k \parallel C_k),
\]

where $C_k$ is the ChaCha20 stream state at epoch $k$.

The runtime guarantees:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Zero hash collisions across all epochs
    \item Deterministic ChaCha20 block evolution
    \item Tamper-evident state propagation
    \item Full auditability of all simulation steps
\end{itemize}

\section{Cross-Node Reproducibility}

Let node $i$ and node $j$ execute the same initial state and timestep:

\[
\mathbf{S}(t_0)_{\text{node}_i}
=
\mathbf{S}(t_0)_{\text{node}_j}.
\]

The runtime guarantees:

\[
\forall k,\quad
\mathbf{S}(t_k)_{\text{node}_i}
=
\mathbf{S}(t_k)_{\text{node}_j}.
\]

This property is validated through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item deterministic arithmetic
    \item deterministic memory access
    \item deterministic vector execution
    \item deterministic cryptographic hashing
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The VS-ACSE deterministic runtime specification defines a fully reproducible,
zero-allocation, vectorized execution model suitable for high-consequence
planetary simulation and distributed scientific computation. By combining
fixed-point arithmetic, AVX2 SIMD parallelism, and cryptographic telemetry,
the runtime achieves absolute determinism across heterogeneous hardware nodes.